IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`

§ `United States — Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 153 and 154`

-- WHAT IS ENCODED, AND FROM WHERE. The Restatement (Second) of Contracts is
-- a copyrighted work of the American Law Institute and is not reproduced from
-- the Institute's own text here. The two sections below are quoted as they
-- appear in a public judicial opinion that adopted them: Donovan v RRL Corp
-- (2001) 26 Cal 4th 261, 27 P 3d 702, in which the Supreme Court of
-- California held: "Because the rule in section 153, subdivision (a), of the
-- Restatement Second of Contracts, authorizing rescission for unilateral
-- mistake of fact where enforcement would be unconscionable, is consistent
-- with our previous decisions, we adopt the rule as California law."
--
-- That court was explicit that adopting it WIDENS the older rule, and the
-- sentence is worth having because it states the American position from the
-- inside: California law "does not adhere to the original Restatement's
-- requirements for rescission based upon unilateral mistake of fact--i.e.,
-- only in circumstances where the other party knew of the mistake or caused
-- the mistake."
--
-- WHAT THAT MEANS FOR THE COMPARISON. The Restatement is not law anywhere
-- until a state adopts it, and states have adopted it unevenly. This module
-- therefore states the Restatement rule, not "the law of the United States",
-- and every row of the comparison table that turns on it should be read that
-- way. Fork F-US1 records the point.
--
-- WHY IT IS THE MOST INSTRUCTIVE COUSIN. Two structural differences from
-- Singapore, and each one changes real outcomes:
--
--   1. It is not confined to a mistake as to a TERM. It reaches "a basic
--      assumption on which he made the contract", which is wider.
--
--   2. Its knowledge limb and its unconscionability limb are ALTERNATIVES,
--      joined by "or". Singapore requires constructive knowledge AND
--      impropriety, conjunctively. So the United States gives relief on
--      constructive knowledge alone -- the very thing Chwee Kin Keong at [80]
--      held insufficient -- and separately gives relief where the other party
--      knew nothing at all, if enforcement would be unconscionable. Donovan
--      itself is the second kind: the buyer "was unaware of the mistake
--      before it was disclosed to him", and the dealer was still let out.

-- TWO DEPARTURES FROM THE PRINTED TEXT, BOTH TYPOGRAPHIC, BOTH DELIBERATE.
-- The quotations of ss 153 and 154 below were string-checked word for word
-- against the opinion as captured in registers/source-bundle.json
-- (donovan-2001), and they match exactly except that:
--
--   * the section sign is transliterated -- the opinion prints "s 154" as
--     "§ 154" -- because an L4 comment is plain source text; and
--   * the opinion's own editorial paragraph markers, printed as "[¶]"
--     before each lettered limb, are dropped.
--
-- Nothing else differs: no word, no punctuation mark, no ordering. This note
-- is here so that a reviewer who diffs the two does not report a discrepancy
-- that was already found and accounted for.

§§ `Section 154 — who bears the risk`

-- "A party bears the risk of a mistake when (a) the risk is allocated to him
-- by agreement of the parties, or (b) he is aware, at the time the contract
-- is made, that he has only limited knowledge with respect to the facts to
-- which the mistake relates but treats his limited knowledge as sufficient,
-- or (c) the risk is allocated to him by the court on the ground that it is
-- reasonable in the circumstances to do so."
--
-- All three limbs are judgements about a particular record, and the third is
-- expressly the court's discretion. This model does not compute them: risk
-- allocation is a fact supplied to it, and each case fixture says which limb
-- it was characterised under and why.

@ref Restatement (Second) of Contracts s 154, as quoted in Donovan v RRL Corp (2001) 26 Cal 4th 261
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mistaken party does not bear the risk` IF
    NOT c's `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake`

§§ `Section 153 — the threshold conditions`

-- "Where a mistake of one party at the time a contract was made as to a basic
-- assumption on which he made the contract has a material effect on the
-- agreed exchange of performances that is adverse to him, the contract is
-- voidable by him if he does not bear the risk of the mistake under the rule
-- stated in s 154, and (a) the effect of the mistake is such that enforcement
-- of the contract would be unconscionable, or (b) the other party had reason
-- to know of the mistake or his fault caused the mistake."
--
-- Note what is NOT asked: whether the mistake was as to a term. A mistake
-- about a term is a mistake about a basic assumption, so both constructors of
-- `Subject of the mistake` clear this threshold. That is deliberate and it is
-- the first divergence.

@ref Restatement (Second) of Contracts s 153, opening words, as quoted in Donovan v RRL Corp
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the threshold conditions of section 153 are met` IF
        c's `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange`
    AND `the mistaken party does not bear the risk` c

§§ `Section 153(a) — unconscionable enforcement`

-- Donovan states the California elements as: "(1) the defendant made a
-- mistake regarding a basic assumption upon which the defendant made the
-- contract; (2) the mistake has a material effect upon the agreed exchange of
-- performances that is adverse to the defendant; (3) the defendant does not
-- bear the risk of the mistake; and (4) the effect of the mistake is such
-- that enforcement of the contract would be unconscionable."
--
-- The other party's state of mind appears nowhere in that list. This is the
-- limb on which the United States relieves a mistaken party against a wholly
-- innocent counterparty, and no other jurisdiction in this corpus does.

@ref Restatement (Second) of Contracts s 153(a); Donovan v RRL Corp (2001) 26 Cal 4th 261
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `enforcement would be unconscionable under section 153(a)` IF
    c's `enforcement would be unconscionable`

§§ `Section 153(b) — reason to know, or fault`

-- Two ways in, either of which suffices. "[H]ad reason to know" is
-- constructive knowledge, and it carries NO additional element of impropriety
-- alongside it -- which is precisely the addition Chwee Kin Keong at [80]
-- held to be required in Singapore.

@ref Restatement (Second) of Contracts s 153(b), as quoted in Donovan v RRL Corp
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the other party had reason to know or was at fault` IF
       `the other party at least had reason to suspect` c
    OR c's `the other party's fault caused the mistake`

§§ `The verdict — voidable by the mistaken party, and never void`

-- Section 153 makes the contract "voidable by him". It does not make it void,
-- and there is no separate common law void limb in this rule. So the United
-- States column of the comparison table never reads `the contract is void`,
-- while Singapore's and England's do -- a difference with direct consequences
-- for anyone who bought from the non-mistaken party in the meantime.

@export Decide what Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 does with a contract one party entered into by mistake — the American rule, under which relief is available on unconscionability alone or on the other party's reason to know alone. States the Restatement rule, not the law of any particular State.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`   @desc The facts of the case, as found, including whether the mistaken party bears the risk under § 154 and whether enforcement would be unconscionable.
GIVETH A `What becomes of the contract`
DECIDE `what the Restatement does` c IS
    IF      `the threshold conditions of section 153 are met` c
        AND (   `enforcement would be unconscionable under section 153(a)` c
             OR `the other party had reason to know or was at fault` c)
    THEN `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
    ELSE `the contract stands`
